1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--functional-fluency","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/functional-fluency","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/functional-fluency","sourceHash":"7e22256983865ab35132d9a7fcbae73c6531bce6eca6fed1af8b7a45fb091c14","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T16:53:01.313Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Functional Fluency","zh":"功能性流利"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Functional fluency is the ability to use the target language for all normal life purposes without it being a significant barrier. You can hold conversations, consume media, handle daily tasks, and express yourself — even if you still make occasional mistakes and lack some specialized vocabulary.","zh":"功能性流利是指能够使用目标语言完成日常生活中的一切常规活动，而这门语言不会构成明显障碍。你可以与人交谈、理解媒体内容、处理日常事务并表达自己——即使你偶尔仍会犯错，也缺少某些专业词汇。"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This is the level we assume you want to reach and the roadmap is written to reflect that. If you have different goals, that's totally fine! You can still use parts of the Refold method, but tailored to your preferences and goals.","zh":"我们默认这是你希望达到的水平，本路线图也据此编写。如果你的目标不同，也完全没问题！你仍然可以采用 Refold 方法的部分内容，并根据自己的偏好和目标进行调整。"}},{"id":"what-it-looks-like","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What It Looks Like","zh":"功能性流利是什么样的"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A functionally fluent person can:","zh":"达到功能性流利的人能够："}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Have extended conversations with native speakers on a wide range of topics","zh":"就广泛的话题与母语者进行长时间交谈"},{"en":"Watch movies and TV shows without subtitles and understand everything important","zh":"不看字幕观看电影和电视剧，并理解所有重要内容"},{"en":"Read books, articles, and social media in the target language","zh":"用目标语言阅读书籍、文章和社交媒体内容"},{"en":"Write emails, messages, and longer texts clearly","zh":"清楚地撰写电子邮件、消息和较长的文本"},{"en":"Handle work, travel, and daily life situations in the language","zh":"用这门语言应对工作、旅行和日常生活中的各种情境"},{"en":"Express opinions, tell stories, explain complex ideas, and make jokes","zh":"表达观点、讲故事、解释复杂概念和开玩笑"}]},{"id":"what-it-doesnt-mean","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"What It Doesn't Mean","zh":"功能性流利不代表什么"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Functional fluency doesn't mean perfection. You'll still make grammar mistakes, search for words, and have vocabulary gaps in specialized areas.","zh":"功能性流利并不意味着完美。你仍会犯语法错误、想不起某些词，并且在专业领域存在词汇缺口。"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You might still have a noticeable foreign accent and native speakers can tell you're not a native speaker. But the language is no longer a barrier — it's just a rougher version of what a native speaker does effortlessly.","zh":"你可能仍带有明显的外国口音，母语者也能听出你并非母语者。但语言已不再构成障碍——你的表现只是比母语者轻松自然的表达稍显生涩。"}},{"id":"when-you-reach-it","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"When You Reach It","zh":"何时能够达到"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Most Refold learners reach functional fluency during Phase 6. This is roughly C1 on the CEFR scale.","zh":"大多数 Refold 学习者会在第 6 阶段达到功能性流利。这大致相当于欧洲语言共同参考框架（CEFR）的 C1 水平。"}},{"id":"beyond-functional-fluency","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Beyond Functional Fluency","zh":"超越功能性流利"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Phase 7 is for learners who want to go beyond functional fluency — toward native-level proficiency, academic expertise, or near-indistinguishable naturalness. Not everyone needs or wants this. Functional fluency is a perfectly valid endpoint.","zh":"第 7 阶段面向希望超越功能性流利的学习者——目标可能是达到母语者水平、具备学术领域的专业能力，或拥有几乎无法与母语者区分的自然表达。并非所有人都需要或想要达到这一阶段。把功能性流利作为终点完全合理。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与原理"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The distinction between \"functional fluency\" and \"native-like proficiency\" reflects an important shift in SLA research. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-1770.1992.tb01044.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cook (1992)</a> introduced the concept of multicompetence — the idea that bilingual and multilingual speakers should be evaluated on their own terms, not against the standard of a monolingual native speaker. This framework legitimizes functional fluency as a meaningful endpoint rather than a deficit relative to native competence.","zh":"“功能性流利”与“接近母语者的熟练程度”之间的区别，反映了第二语言习得研究中的一次重要转变。<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-1770.1992.tb01044.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cook（1992）</a>提出了“多元语言能力”的概念——双语及多语使用者应该根据其自身特点接受评价，而不是以单语母语者为标准。该框架承认功能性流利本身就是有意义的终点，而不是相较于母语能力的一种缺陷。"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<a href=\"https://www.multilingual-matters.com/page/detail/Approaches-to-Second-Language-Acquisition/?k=9781853592348\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Towell &amp; Hawkins (1994)</a> identified \"incompleteness\" as one of the central phenomena of SLA — most learners' grammars stabilize before fully converging on native norms, yet these learners remain highly functional communicators. Functional fluency — the ability to do everything you need to do in the language — is where the overwhelming majority of language learners plateau, and research supports that this is both realistic and sufficient for most purposes.","zh":"<a href=\"https://www.multilingual-matters.com/page/detail/Approaches-to-Second-Language-Acquisition/?k=9781853592348\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Towell &amp; Hawkins（1994）</a>将“不完整性”视为第二语言习得的核心现象之一——大多数学习者的语法体系会在完全趋同于母语规范之前趋于稳定，但这些学习者仍然能够高效交流。绝大多数语言学习者最终都会停留在功能性流利——能够用这门语言完成自己需要做的一切——这一水平；研究也表明，对大多数用途而言，这一目标既现实又足够。"}},{"id":"segment-0016","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The CEFR scale uses C1 as its penultimate level, defined by the ability to understand and express oneself effectively across a wide range of topics. This alignment between functional fluency and C1 reflects a broad consensus in applied linguistics that distinguishes very high proficiency (C1) from near-native proficiency (C2), acknowledging that C2 requires thousands of additional hours and is rarely necessary outside specialized professional, academic contexts or truly obsessed learners.","zh":"CEFR 量表将 C1 设为倒数第二个等级，其定义是在广泛的话题中有效理解他人并表达自己的能力。功能性流利与 C1 的对应，反映了应用语言学领域的广泛共识：非常高的熟练程度（C1）不同于接近母语者的熟练程度（C2）。达到 C2 还需要额外投入数千小时；除专业工作、学术环境或真正痴迷于语言学习的人之外，很少有人有此必要。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
